const RULES: [string, string][] = [
  ['Finding your way around', 'The menu on the left is on every screen and lists only what your roles allow, grouped by what you are doing: My work, Members, Teller and cash, Loans, Accounting, Purchasing and sales, Reports, Compliance and records, Administration. The arrows at its top fold it to icons. Home shows the same screens as tiles, and Ctrl+K searches them (and members). All three read one list of screens, so they always agree. A tab inside a screen, such as Accounting > Debit/credit memos, has its own entry.'],
  ['Waiting for you', 'The first entry of the menu, and the card at the top of Home, list everything that needs your approval or action: member profile changes and member requests (for Membership Officers), journals and memos to review or post (accountants), purchase requests and orders (purchase approvers), stock adjustments (stock approvers) and extra workflow steps. It never lists your own work, because someone else must decide it. Each item opens the screen that decides it, on the right tab; profile changes, member requests and workflow steps are decided on the same page, below the list.'],
  ['Back Office', 'The Back Office tile (accountants and administrators) is one entry window, laid out like the old General Journal Transactions window. Choose the Trans. type on top: the list has every back-office type of the old system, with its code and how often the old system used it. General Journal and Debit/Credit Memo are made here, and Penalty Waiver, at the end, lists the waiver journals; choosing any other type says it is not made in this application yet, or where it is made (a reversing entry with Reverse…, the closing entry by the year-end close, a time deposit rollover on TD Maturities). The toolbar has Find (search the entries of that type by control number, description, who made it or status, and sort them with Sort by, the arrow beside it, or a column heading; it opens on the date, newest first), New, Save, Cancel (asks before throwing away what you typed), and Previous and Next, which Page Up and Page Down also do. In the lists and on an open entry, where it stands is shown as steps, like the loan applications: 1 Draft, 2 Review (approval by someone other than the maker), 3 Posting, a check for each step done and a ring at the one it waits at; a posted entry shows every step checked and Posted, a cancelled memo says Cancelled. An entry shows its control number, date and who made, approved and posted it, the GL/SL entries with the debit and credit totals and the difference, and the description; the steps (Send for review, Approve, Post, Reverse) are under it. Each entry is made by one person and approved by another; a memo is posted by a third. The books themselves (trial balance, financial statements, periods, banks and the year-end close) are on the Accounting tile.'],
  ['Disbursement vouchers', 'Back Office, tab Disbursement Transactions (like the old window of that name), or Menu > Accounting > Disbursement vouchers. The Trans. type list has every disbursement type of the old system; Cash Voucher and Check Voucher are made here, and Loan Voucher, Deposit Withdrawal and Misc Disbursement say where they are made. Enter the member (by ID), for a Check Voucher the And/Or (the payee on the cheque, the member unless you type another name), the bank account, cheque number and date, then the debits and the description. The window adds the credit itself: the payee’s vouchers payable for a Cash Voucher, the bank for a Check Voucher. Save and send for approval encodes it with every check the front office uses; another person approves it and a third posts it (Approve and Post on the voucher, or Front Office > Disburse > Vouchers waiting). A Cash Voucher is then paid out on Front Office > Disburse. Making one needs New on that type (Settings > Users > Transactions). The list shows the vouchers waiting; posted ones are under Front Office.'],
  ['Debit/credit memos', 'The Back Office tile with Trans. type Debit/Credit Memo, or Menu > Accounting > Debit/credit memos (accountants and administrators): an adjustment or transfer that changes members’ savings or share capital accounts and ledger accounts in one balanced entry, for example from a deceased member to a beneficiary. A debit takes money out of a member’s account, a credit puts it in. It must give the reason and who authorised it, and it can never take a member’s account below zero. The person who makes it, the person who approves it and the person who posts it must be three different people. A posted memo is reversed by a new memo, never edited. Loans are not included yet.'],
  ['Where an entry came from', 'In the details of a Savings, Share Capital, Time Deposit or Loan account, click a ledger row (or press Enter on it) to see the transaction behind it: the header, the general ledger and subsidiary ledger lines it wrote (with their totals), the cheques it carried, the source reference and the description. It only opens transactions that are in that member’s own ledger, and nothing in it can be changed.'],
  ['Know Your Customer (KYC)', 'Open a member, then Member info > KYC. Record the valid ID the member showed (kind, number, who issued it, expiry date, and a scan if you may handle images), where their money comes from, whether they hold or held a public position (politically exposed), a risk rating (low, medium or high) and the date the record is next reviewed; left blank, the review date is set from the risk (a year for high, two for medium, three for low). The status shows at the top of the section and as a tag in the side panel: Not started, Incomplete (something needed is missing), ID expired, Review due, or Complete. Saving needs permission to edit members, applies at once (it does not wait for a second person) and is written to the audit log with the ID number hidden except its last four characters; opening an ID scan is logged too.'],
  ['DOSRI tag', 'Member profile > Member info > Membership > DOSRI: whether the member is one of the cooperative’s directors, officers or staff, or a related interest of one, chosen from the cooperative’s DOSRI list (— for none). Changed in edit mode and sent for approval like any profile edit (a Membership Officer’s own edit applies at once). A tagged member shows a DOSRI tag under their name in the side panel. The aging report can group by it.'],
  ['KYC review', 'Menu > Members > KYC review (people who may edit members, Membership Officers and administrators). Active members whose KYC needs attention, one kind at a time with the count of each: ID expired, Incomplete, Review due, No record. Click a member to open their profile on the KYC section and put it right; the list updates when you save.'],
  ['KYC and withdrawals', 'The teller sees the member’s KYC standing under Member information. By default it is only a reminder. An administrator can switch on Settings > Membership > Membership policy > Withdrawals need a good KYC record (with a reason, audited): then savings withdrawals, share capital withdrawals and time deposit pay-outs and interest withdrawals are refused for a member with no KYC record, an incomplete one or an expired ID, until it is put right. An overdue review never stops a withdrawal. Look at the KYC review list before switching it on, since most members have no record yet.'],
  ['Editing a profile', 'A person with edit permission changes a member in edit mode and saves; the change is sent to a Membership Officer, who is not the sender, to approve or reject. A person who is a Membership Officer does not need a second person: their changes are applied at once when they save. Either way every changed field is written to the audit log (Settings > Compliance and Audit > Audit Log) with who made it, the old and new value and, for an officer’s own edit, a note that no second approval was needed.'],
  ['Beneficiaries', 'Member Profile > Info > Beneficiaries (right of Spouse Info). A person with edit permission adds, changes or removes a beneficiary by sending a request; a Membership Officer other than the sender approves or rejects it on the Approvals tile, and only then does the list change. A removal needs a reason, and the removed details are kept in the Audit Log.'],
  ['Finding a member', 'A search by name lists active members only, so it is quicker. Turn on Include inactive in Find Member to see dormant and closed ones. A member ID or reference number finds the member whatever the status. The title-bar search and the pickers in Front Office, Loan Application and co-makers list active members only. A word that starts a last or first name is found at once; a word from the middle of a name or account name takes a moment longer. When more than a page matches, the list says so instead of counting them.'],
  ['Posted money entries never change', 'A mistake is fixed with a reversal and a corrected entry, never by editing. Every entry balances (debits equal credits) before it posts.'],
  ['Maker and checker', 'The person who asks is never the person who approves. This holds for profile changes, new members, closings, reactivations, journals, loans, purchases and stock adjustments.'],
  ['Everything is audited', 'Who, when (to the millisecond), what changed (each field before and after) and why. Administrators read it under Settings > Compliance and Audit > Audit Log. Passwords, fingerprint data and other secrets are never written to it.'],
  ['Changes to your access apply at once', 'When an administrator changes what you may do (Settings > Users), it applies from your next action; you do not sign out. Within about 15 seconds your window says what changed and who changed it, and your menu and buttons are updated. Anything you were posting when it changed finishes normally. If your account is switched off or your password is reset, you are signed out and the sign-in screen says why.'],
  ['One business day at a time', 'Teller and front-office postings carry the branch’s open business date, not the computer’s date, and while no business day is open, nothing can be done but look: no deposits, withdrawals, payments, vouchers, drafts, journals, memos, penalty waivers, POS sales or returns, stock movements, goods received, depreciation or the year-end close (a notice across the top of every screen says so, with a button to Business Day; the title bar shows Posting closed). Quotes, checks, previews, printed documents and passbook printing still work, and so do settings, members, loan applications, users and reports. A day is never later than today and always after the last closed day; Sundays and holidays must be confirmed. End Day checks that every teller who handled cash counted and closed the drawer, no cash hand-off is waiting and every voucher of the day balances; with a check failing, the day can be ended only with a reason, which is kept with the day. Journals and memos keep the date the accountant chooses, but are posted only while a day is open; interest, accrual and dividend runs keep their period-end date and need an open day too.'],
  ['Posting asks first', 'Posting a journal, memo, dividend or accrual voucher, a loan release, a returned cheque, a penalty waiver or the year-end close, closing or locking a period, and approving a whole list of new members first show what will happen (the amount, who it affects, the date) and wait for you to confirm. Back leaves everything as it was.'],
  ['Closed periods take no postings', 'Nothing is posted dated in a year whose books are closed, or in an accounting period that is soft-closed, closed or locked: not teller deposits, withdrawals and payments, loan releases, cash handovers, time deposit pay-outs, savings interest, time deposit accruals or dividends. The message names the period; an accountant can reopen a soft-closed one under Accounting > Periods. Days that no period covers are not blocked; journals and memos need an open period.'],
  ['Nothing is deleted', 'A member is closed, not removed; a certificate, loan or voucher is reversed or marked, not erased.'],
  ['The old database is protected', 'The application only adds or updates the few things it is allowed to, and does so through limited database rights.'],
]

const WHERE: [string, string][] = [
  ['Start Day and End Day', 'Menu > Teller and cash > Business Day, or press the date in the title bar. Start Day (the Start Day right, or an administrator) opens the suggested date: today, or the latest working day after the last closed one. End Day (the End Day right, or an administrator) shows its checks; settle what failed, or give the reason for ending anyway. An administrator can reopen the last closed day, with a reason, while no day is open. The rights are in Settings > Users, tab Transactions, Business day. Every step is in the day’s history and the audit log. End Day first checks, and refuses at once if something must be settled or explained; then it runs the end-of-day jobs (a minute or two): balances of every drawer and account type, the aging of every open loan with the formula’s penalty, time deposits matured and not paid out, interest runs that are due (encoded to wait for approval, as when run by hand), and cheques past their clearing day. Nothing is posted by the jobs. A step that fails is kept and tried again; Run the jobs now on the Business Day screen runs them before End Day.'],
  ['Opening your drawer', 'Front Office > Teller, or Cash Handover: Open my drawer. At the start of the business day each teller and cashier counts their own drawer, by note and coin, before taking or paying out cash; the figure the system expected is shown only after the count. A difference needs a reason and is kept; the drawer opens either way, and the count cannot be changed afterwards. Until it is opened, cash at the window waits. The head cashier sees every drawer opened that day. An administrator can switch the rule off on the Business Day screen, with a reason.'],
  ['Vouchers and other entries from a draft', 'Front Office: make the entry as a draft, save it, then Post…, which shows exactly what will be written. A Cash Receipt for something that is not a member’s account (a fee, rent, a collection for someone else) and a Misc Disbursement paying a member’s vouchers payable or a ledger account are posted at once on your drawer. A Cash Voucher (paid out later on Disburse) and a Check Voucher (paid by the cooperative’s own cheque: choose the bank account, cheque number, date and payee) wait on Disburse > Vouchers waiting: another person approves, a third posts. A cheque number is never used twice on the same bank account. Member deposits, withdrawals and payments stay on the Teller tab.'],
  ['Printing a passbook', 'Front Office > Passbook: find the member and choose the savings account. The lines the next page will print are shown with their line numbers, starting after the last line printed (the same record the old system kept, so nothing is printed twice). Print page, check the paper, then press Yes, printed right: only then does the passbook move on. A full page continues at line 1 of the next page. A jammed page is printed again with Reprint; a full or lost passbook is replaced with New passbook (the head cashier or an administrator), which starts with the balance forwarded. Layout sets where the lines fall on the 5½ × 8 inch passbook, with a test page to line it up; administrators save it. Every print, reprint and change is kept in the passbook’s history.'],
  ['Net surplus appropriation', 'Accounting > Dividends > Appropriation (accountants and administrators), once the year is closed: the year’s net surplus (what the closing put in the undivided net surplus) is first set aside to the statutory funds, each a percent of it, as the Cooperative Code (RA 9520, Article 86) requires: reserve fund at least 10% (at least 50% in the first five years after registration; an administrator records the registration date there), education and training fund at most 10%, community development fund at least 3%, optional fund at most 7%. Then the amounts the general assembly allocated to interest on share capital and patronage refund, credited to the account the dividend vouchers pay from. What is not allocated stays undivided. Name the resolution; it is sent for review, approved by another person and posted by a third as one General Journal. The year’s distribution then pays members no more than appropriated; Prepare has Fill in from the posted appropriation.'],
  ['Dividends and patronage refund', 'Accounting > Dividends. Prepare: choose the cut-off and either type the two rates or enter the net surplus, the statutory funds and the amounts the general assembly allocated; review the figures and the split, then Prepare for review, which stores every member’s figures. Distributions: submit it; someone else approves it (or rejects it with a reason); only then Make the vouchers, from exactly the approved figures; a second person approves each voucher and a third posts it. Preparing a year again replaces a version not yet approved, and every version is kept. Each distribution has its reports (net surplus, statutory funds, interest on share capital, patronage refund, ledger posting, audit and reconciliation), the register and member statements. Settings (administrators): which loan charges count as patronage, who takes part by status, which member types earn interest and which a refund, a minimum average share, and the statutory funds.'],
  ['Cash deposits and withdrawals', 'Only in the Front Office, on the Teller tab: savings deposits and withdrawals, share capital payments and withdrawals, loan payments, and time deposit placement, interest withdrawal and pre-termination. A member\'s profile only shows the accounts. A savings withdrawal is refused when it is more than the cash in the teller\'s own drawer: ask the head cashier for cash first (Cash Handover).'],
  ['Closing the year', 'Menu > Accounting > Year-end close, or the Year-end close tab of Accounting (accountants and administrators), from 1 January of the next year. Choose the year to see its income, expenses and surplus by department. Prepare closing records it; another accountant posts it. Posting writes one Closing Entry dated 31 December, as the old system does: every income and expense account goes to zero in each department and the difference goes to 30198 Undivided net surplus. The year’s accounting periods close, and nothing can be posted into the year after that (journals, memos, teller transactions, interest, accruals and dividends); correct a mistake with a journal dated in an open year. Years close in order, and not while a journal of the year is unposted. If anything is booked into the year after it was prepared, posting is refused: cancel it with a reason and prepare it again. Sharing out the surplus (reserve fund, education and training fund, community development fund, optional fund, interest on share capital and patronage refund) is done afterwards with journals.'],
  ['Loan applications', 'In the Loan Module tile: New loan application starts one, and Loan applications waiting lists those in progress. Each goes through 1 · Profiling, 2 · Deduction, 3 · Verification and 4 · Approval there, by the people who have each step (Settings > Users, Receivables, Loan process). 5 · Loan Release, releasing the money, is not in the Loan Module: it is in Front Office > Disburse (Loan releases waiting, then Pay out vouchers payable), by those allowed to release money (the Cash Voucher right). The member’s Loans tab shows loans and their profile. The Term starts at the loan type’s term, which is also the longest it allows. Print amortization and Next (on Profiling) can be pressed once its required fields are filled in; until then, hovering over them says what is still needed. Print amortization prints the schedule of what is on the form, with a page for the member to sign; it saves nothing.'],
  ['Members', 'Member Profile: Info (Membership, Personal, Business / Employment, Education, Contacts & IDs, Spouse Info), Accounts (Share Capital, Savings, Time Deposits, Loans, Billing, Pledges / Insurances) and Other. Register a new member with Member Profile menu > New (Ctrl+N; the form opens blank), close or reactivate one from the profile; an officer other than the requester approves under Approvals.'],
  ['Registering many members', 'Advanced mode, for Membership Officers only (an administrator assigns the role in Settings > Users): on the Member Profile finder screen choose Advanced mode: import a list. Upload a CSV or Excel file (download the blank Excel list: its Guide sheet explains each column), see every row checked, and send the valid ones. A Membership Officer other than the uploader approves the whole list under Approvals, which opens the members. Photos and fingerprints are added afterwards on each member.'],
  ['Adding an account to the chart', 'Settings > Accounting > Chart of Accounts. The cards at the top count every account, the posting accounts in use, the active and the switched-off ones; press one to filter. Below, the accounts as a hierarchy with their type, normal balance (debit or credit), posting (heading, journal entries, or subsidiary ledger) and status; search, filter by type, status or posting, and Export the list as CSV. New account (or + on a heading) opens a panel on the right: 01 the name, an optional description and the account code; 02 the type, the heading it goes under (Suggested shows likely headings from the name) and the normal balance; 03 whether it takes postings and journal entries. The code is generated from the heading, the next free number under it, and can be typed instead (Auto-generate goes back). The normal balance follows the type (debit for assets and expenses, credit for the rest); choosing the other side makes it a contra account, such as an allowance for losses. A number or a name already used under the heading is refused as you type. Every save asks for a reason; the History tab of an account shows who changed what, when and why. An account is switched off, never deleted.'],
  ['Adding many accounts to the chart', 'Advanced mode, for administrators: on Chart of Accounts choose Upload from Excel. Upload a CSV or Excel file (download the blank Excel list: its Guide sheet explains each column and has an example), see what every row will do, give a reason and add. A list only adds accounts: a number already in the chart is left as it is, and nothing is renamed, moved or deleted. The accounts are added together or not at all, each one written to the audit log.'],
  ['Account types', 'Settings > Products, for administrators: the savings, time deposit, share capital, receivable and payable types (the old S/L Type Table) and the chart accounts each one posts to. Change a type’s names, order, interest rate, minimum and maximum balance and dormancy period, switch it off, add a type as a copy of another, or point a ledger link at another account of the same kind. When choosing the account for a link (or for a first type), Suggested shows up to three: what the class’s other types use for the same posting, then accounts of the right kind whose names match; hover over one to see why, press it to fill it in. Delete type removes a type nothing was ever opened or posted under (one added by mistake), with its ledger links; a type that was used cannot be deleted and is switched off instead, so its history can still be read. Every change needs a reason and is kept in the audit log. Loan types are changed in Loan Setup.'],
  ['Savings dormancy', 'Settings > Membership > Savings dormancy. Administrators see the savings accounts with no deposit or withdrawal for the period set on their type (interest and dividends do not count), choose some and mark them dormant with a reason. A dormant account takes no deposit or withdrawal. Administrators and Membership Officers find a dormant account by member number or name and reactivate it with a reason. Nothing is marked by itself.'],
  ['Loan deductions', 'Settings > Products > Loan products, open a loan type, tab Deductions: the lines an application of that type starts with and where each goes (a member account, a charge of the loan, or a chart account). Add, change or remove a line with a reason; the amount is typed on the application. The net proceeds line always stays last.'],
  ['Member profile layout', 'Member Profile has a dark panel on the left (photo, name, the fingerprint, signature and privacy-notice tags and the main menu: Member info, Accounts, Other; pressing one opens its sections as a sub-menu, for example Membership, Personal and Business / Employment under Member info), the open section’s name across the top with member type, department, date opened and, beside them, the account status and alert level, under Accounts, four balance cards (share capital, savings, loans owed, time deposits; press one to open its section; they are not shown on the other menus) and the section itself below. Nothing was removed from the old layout; the same fields are in the same sections.'],
  ['Member photo', 'The camera icon on the photo, shown only in edit mode, to people with both the edit-member and edit-member-images permissions. Photos are added, never overwritten.'],
  ['Member signature', 'The Signature tag beside Fingerprint in the left panel of the profile. Pressing it shows the signature on file. People with both the edit-member and edit-member-images permissions also get Capture signature (or Replace signature): the member signs on the pad with a mouse, finger or pen, or a scanned signature is chosen from a file, then Save signature. Signatures are added, never overwritten; the newest is shown.'],
  ['Fingerprints', 'The Fingerprint tag under the photo. The member\'s consent comes first and can be withdrawn, which erases every fingerprint. Until a scanner is connected a simulator is used for testing only.'],
  ['Loan Setup', 'Settings > Products > Loan products opens in a window of its own. Tabs: loan types, purposes, collateral, co-maker holds and amortization. Opening a loan type shows Setup and COA Links; an administrator can change, add or remove its links to the chart of accounts there (principal, interest, penalty and fees), with a reason, audited. New postings use the new account.'],
  ['Backup', 'Settings > System > Backup (administrators): Back up now saves every table the application owns and the fingerprint key into a dated folder on the server; the list shows past backups and marks one Incomplete if the dump or the key is missing. The old system’s tables are not in it. Copy the dump and the key together to another computer. Restoring is done by hand from the dump, not from this screen.'],
  ['Database connection', 'Sign-in screen > Connection: pick which server the app talks to (a list kept on this computer) and see which database it works on. Settings > System > Database connection (SUPERADMIN only): save connections (name, host, port, database, branch), then Use this connection to move the server to one; everyone signs in again. The database user and password stay in the server’s settings.'],
  ['Sign-in screen wording', 'Settings > Organization > Sign-in screen and branding (SUPERADMIN only): the cooperative’s logo, name and tagline shown on the left of the sign-in screen, with a live preview. Everyone sees them, including people not signed in yet.'],
  ['Loan worksheet', 'Member Profile > Loans > open a loan > Worksheet as of (a date), then Worksheet PDF or Worksheet Excel: the Loan Interest and Penalty Computation Worksheet. Each download is recorded in the audit log and the export log. The penalty columns are not yet confirmed against a late loan.'],
  ['Creating a user', 'Settings > Users > New user (administrators): username, name, branch and level, and optionally Copy permissions from another user, who lends their permissions, per-transaction rights and roles but never their name, password or member number. Create user shows a temporary password once; give it to the person, who chooses their own at first sign-in. Only a super administrator can create an administrator. Change the new user’s rights afterwards by selecting them in the list.'],
  ['Cash types and drawers', 'Settings > Accounting > Cash and teller drawers (administrators). A new database has no cash types: Set up the cash types adds the system’s own (cash on hand, cash in bank, cheques and other cash items, ATM items) and links the vault and the cheques on hand to the chart accounts you choose. The first teller’s or cashier’s drawer asks which accounts its cash and cheques post to; later drawers of that kind take the same. Give the drawer number to the person in Settings > Users.'],
  ['Product roles', 'Settings > Products > Product roles (administrators): which of this cooperative’s own account types the system posts to by itself. Vouchers payable is the fund and payable type that receives a loan’s net proceeds and a cash or check voucher, and is paid out at the teller. Each cooperative chooses its own; until it does, loan applications, vouchers and pay-outs say so and point here. Changes ask for a reason and are kept in the audit log.'],
  ['SL Controls', 'Settings > Products > Account opening (SL Controls) (administrators): which savings, fund and share capital accounts open by themselves, empty, when a Membership Officer approves a new member or reactivates one, with a switch per account type and member type. Change the switches, give a reason and Save changes; each change goes to the audit log. Every member type keeps one share capital account, and a type switched off in Account Types cannot be switched on. The first deposit and share payment are still taken at the teller.'],
  ['Features', 'Settings > System > Features: each database is its own cooperative, and not every cooperative offers savings, time deposits, loans or a store. A super administrator switches off what this cooperative does not offer, with a reason kept in the audit log. An area that is off disappears from the menu, tiles, search, member profile and teller window, members are no longer given its accounts, and its actions are refused. Nothing is deleted: switching it back on brings everything back. Open windows update within about 15 seconds.'],
  ['Member defaults', 'Settings > Membership > Member defaults: the department and office a new member is given when the registration does not say, and which member types this cooperative registers. A super administrator chooses them, with a reason. If one is not chosen, registration says so and where to choose it. The accounts a new member gets are chosen in Settings > Products > Account opening (SL Controls); a member type with nothing chosen there cannot be registered.'],
  ['Database changes', 'A new version of the application can need changes to the database (new tables, or permissions for the application). When the connected database lacks some, administrators see a red notice on Settings and on Settings > System > Database connection naming them. A super administrator can press Apply them now and give the database administrator’s login (usually coop_admin) and a reason: the changes are applied one at a time, a change that fails is undone and stops the rest, and each one is kept in the audit log. The login is used once and is not saved.'],
  ['Departments', 'Settings > Organization > Departments (administrators): the cooperative’s departments (the main office, satellites, buildings), each optionally under a consolidating one, with its TIN and address. Members, users and the books are kept by department, and a new member is given the default department from Settings > Membership > Member defaults when none is chosen. Add a department, change it, or switch it off when it is no longer used; one nothing ever used can be deleted, and department 2 always stays. Every change asks for a reason, kept in the audit log.'],
  ['Auditors', 'An administrator gives a person the Auditor role in Settings > Users, Roles. An auditor opens Accounting (trial balance, statements, periods, banks, year-end close, budget), the Chart of Accounts, Reports (including the Journal listing, the General ledger and Fixed assets against the ledger, each printed or exported as CSV) and the Audit Log, and can change nothing.'],
  ['Tracing a figure', 'On the trial balance and on the Statements of Financial Condition and of Operations, an account code is a link: it opens that account’s entries for the period, with the opening balance and the balance after each entry. A journal or memo in Back Office keeps its supporting documents (receipts, invoices, approvals) under Supporting documents.'],
  ['Closing the books', 'Accounting > Month-end review: pick a month and go through its checklist. The system checks that the books balance, entries are posted, banks are reconciled, depreciation is run and business days are ended; an accountant confirms the SL reconciliation, the cash count and that material adjustments are posted. Anything the system finds wanting is accepted only with an explanation. Sign off the month when every item is done. Monthly closing is optional: an administrator decides in the Closing policy whether signing a month off also closes it. Accounting > Year-end close is mandatory: the year-end checklist (the month items plus accruals, the allowance for loan losses, prepayments, fixed assets and inventory) must be complete before the closing can be prepared by one accountant and posted by another. Accounting warns when the year is still open, and when its close is overdue (by default 120 days after 31 December; the Closing policy sets it).'],
  ['Opening balances', 'Accounting > Opening balances (accountants and administrators): what the cooperative has on its first day on this system. Prepare a batch: ledger lines for cash, banks, fixed assets, payables and equity, and member lines for each member’s savings and share capital (a credit), loans still being paid (a debit: what is still owed, with the status, the months still to run, the next due date and the rate; posting opens the loan with its remaining schedule) and time deposits still running (a credit: the principal, with the placement date, term in days, rate and the interest earned up to the opening date, which goes in as the certificate’s accrued interest; posting opens the certificate, numbered by the system). Do not type the control accounts (members’ savings, paid-up share capital, loans receivable, time deposits): the system adds the members into them, so the members’ accounts and the books agree. For many members, Download template, fill it in and Import from a file. The batch shows the trial balance it makes and what is still to fix; it is sent for review, approved by another person and posted by a third as one General Journal on the opening date (an open business day and accounting period are needed). An account that already has entries takes no opening balance; after posting, correct a member’s balance with a debit/credit memo and a ledger balance with a journal. Member funds and payables are not entered here yet.'],
  ['Budget', 'Accounting > Budget: the annual budget of each income and expense account, beside last year’s actual. Type the amounts and Save changes with one reason; each change is kept in the audit log. Budget versus actual sets the budget for a period (spread evenly over the months) against the ledger: green ▲ is favourable (revenue above budget, expense below), red ▼ unfavourable.'],
  ['The four financial statements', 'Accounting > Statements, Statement: Financial Condition (as at a date), Operations (for a period), Changes in Equity (each equity account from the start of the period to its end: what came in, what went out; the current year’s surplus is its own row) and Cash Flows (the indirect method: net surplus, then the change in each balance-sheet account by class, operating, investing or financing, explaining the change in cash). Compare with the end of last year (Financial Condition) or with the same period last year (Operations) puts the prior figures beside this period’s, with the change. Cash flow classes (on the Statement of Cash Flows) shows the class of each balance-sheet heading; the accounts under it follow it. Suggested classes come from the headings’ names; an accountant or administrator changes one with a reason, kept in the audit log. A line Not explained appears only where old entries do not balance.'],
  ['Statements by unit and consolidated', 'Accounting > Statements: choose the Unit. Whole branch is every unit together; a main office, branch or satellite office gives that unit with the units under it (its buildings, for example); a consolidation gives the sum of the units under it. Show each unit side by side puts each unit under a consolidation in its own column, then the entries posted to the consolidation itself (its adjustments and eliminations), then the consolidated total. What each department is (main office, branch, satellite office, consolidation, other unit) is set in Settings > Organization > Departments, column Kind; until it is set, a kind is suggested from the name. A statement as at a date needs the earlier years summed: an administrator presses Build balances on the Statements tab once (it runs in the background).'],
  ['Offices', 'Settings > Organization > Offices (administrators): the cooperative’s offices (the main office and its satellites). Every member belongs to one, and a new member is given office 1 when none is chosen, so a database needs office 1 before members can be registered. Each user is given the offices they work for in Settings > Users. Add an office with its number and name, rename it, or delete one no member or user belongs to; office 1 always stays. Every change asks for a reason, kept in the audit log. The number stays with the office for good.'],
  ['Setting up a new database', 'A new database starts without the standard lists the system works with (account statuses, terms, payment modes, genders, civil statuses and others), so screens such as Products and New Member cannot work yet. An administrator opens Settings > System > Starter setup, gives a reason and loads the missing rows. Only missing rows are added; nothing already there is changed. Then add the departments and offices in Settings > Organization, choose the Member defaults and the Features this cooperative uses, and set up the chart of accounts and the products in Settings > Products.'],
  ['When something fails', 'If the system cannot do something because of a problem on its side, the screen says in everyday words what could not be done and what you can do, and gives a reference such as E-261121-AB3K. Give that reference to your administrator: in Settings > System > Error log they find, under it, the technical cause, what to do about it, who met it and when. Refusals that are about your request (a missing reason, a member who cannot be registered yet) say who and why, whether anything was saved, and the ways to finish.'],
  ['Registering a member with one officer', 'A new member is normally registered by one person and approved by a Membership Officer other than them, so that a second person checks every new member. Where a cooperative has a single officer, a super administrator can switch on “Membership Officers approve their own registrations” in Settings > Membership > Membership policy, with a reason (kept in the audit log). Then a registration sent by a Membership Officer opens the member at once, with the accounts Account opening (SL Controls) names; the screen says so before sending and the button reads Register member. Each such registration is recorded in the audit log as registered directly. A registration the officer sent before the switch was on stays waiting, but appears in their Waiting for you list and they can approve it themselves on Approvals. Anyone who is not a Membership Officer still waits for one, and closing, reactivating and beneficiary changes still need a second officer. Off by default.'],
  ['Journals and control accounts', 'A control account is a chart account that members’ accounts post to, such as Savings Deposit, Paid-up share capital or a loan type’s current and past due accounts; it must always equal the total of those members’ accounts (Reports > SL reconciliation checks it). A journal changes the general ledger only, never a member’s account, so it may not change a control account’s balance: it is refused, with the accounts named. To change members’ balances use a debit/credit memo, which changes the member’s account and the control account together. A journal may still move an amount between control accounts of the same members’ accounts so that they net to zero, for example a loan from current to past due. An administrator can make one that changes a control account by ticking “This journal changes a control account directly” and saying why (an opening balance, an approved correction); the reason is kept in the audit log, and posting checks it again. Reversing a posted journal is always allowed. The old system allowed such journals freely; it used them for the 2023 opening balances and, in 2025, to force the ledger to the members’ totals, which hides a difference instead of finding it.'],
  ['SL reconciliation', 'Menu > Reports > SL reconciliation, or Reports > Accounting (administrators and accountants): choose the branch and the date and press Run (it adds up every member’s ledger, so it takes a few minutes; a run from the last ten minutes is shown again, Run again works it out afresh). Each control account in the chart is compared with the total of the members’ accounts that post to it: savings, share capital, loans, time deposits, payables and funds. A loan type is compared with all its status accounts together (current, past due, restructured, in litigation), and types that share an account are compared together. Groups that do not reconcile come first, in red, opened to their types and accounts; the difference is the ledger less the members. A difference means an entry reached one ledger and not the other, for example a journal posted straight to a control account, or a member’s row typed with a wrong amount. Nothing is changed by it; print it or save it as CSV.'],
  ['Aging of receivables', 'Menu > Reports > Aging of receivables, or Reports > Receivables (administrators and accountants): choose the cut-off date and how to age, PAR @ days (how late the oldest unpaid instalment is) or Maturity @ days (days past maturity); narrow it by release, maturity and last transaction dates, SL class and type, balance and days; group it up to three levels (for example SL type, then collector, then aging bucket) and sort the accounts. Show totals only leaves the accounts out. The Accrued interest tab adds the interest owed on the cut-off date (worked out as on the loan’s interest and penalty worksheet; interest taken in advance at release counts as paid), the interest paid ahead, a penalty estimate and the total owed, and can list only the accounts that owe some. Click a group to fold or open it. Excel gives a workbook whose groups fold with Excel’s + and − buttons; PDF gives legal paper, landscape, with the summary on top; CSV and Print are there too. The files hold the run on screen. Your choices are remembered on this computer.'],
  ['CIC submission', 'Menu > Compliance and records > CIC submission, or Settings > Compliance and Audit > CIC submission (administrators and accountants): make a batch, generate it from the books at the cut-off date, read the findings, have another person approve it, download the file and record the CIC’s reference, result and reconciliation. Approval stays off until the file mapping is confirmed by a file the CIC accepts.'],
  ['Data privacy','Menu > Compliance and records > Data privacy, or Settings > Compliance and Audit > Data privacy (administrators and data protection officers): log a request about someone’s data, check identity, hand over the member data file, erase fingerprints; incidents with a 72-hour reporting deadline; sharing, assessments, notices, retention and classification; the export log. The privacy notice is recorded on the member profile. Starting retention rules and classifications are drafts to confirm against the Data Privacy Act.'],
  ['Audit log and its seals', 'Menu > Compliance and records > Audit Log, or Settings > Compliance and Audit > Audit Log (administrators): every change with who, what, when, where (address and device), why, before and after. The banner shows how far the log is sealed; the log is sealed every night, each seal chained to the one before, and Check the seals reports any entry that was changed or removed. Nothing in the app can edit or delete an audit entry.'],
  ['Settings', 'Administrator controls: users and permissions, the audit log, membership policy, approval workflows, documents, loan setup and the chart of accounts.'],
]

